  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Monsoon activity continues this\nafternoon as the upper ridge near the four corners keeps bringing\nin moisture from the south into Nevada. Chances for isolated to\nscattered showers and thunderstorms (20-50%) across central Nevada\nalong the US-50 corridor and into northeastern Nevada. Humboldt\nCounty expected to remain dry. Storms are expected to be wet, but\nas storm motions increase today, storms will become a mix of wet\nand dry. Monday, models still show a 20-50% chance for isolated to\nscattered thunderstorms and will be more across northern Nevada\nand parts of White Pine County. Due to storm motions being a\nlittle fast, much of the storms will be a mix of wet and dry,\nespecially for Humboldt county starting as dry thunderstorms and\ntransition to more of a mix by the evening. By Tuesday and\nWednesday, thunderstorm chances continue to drop to a 10-30%\nchance more across eastern Nevada. Greatest hazards with these\nthunderstorms is gusty erratic outflow winds up to 45-55 mph,\nbrief periods of heavy rainfall especially over recently burned\nareas, and cloud to ground lightning. The cooling temperature\ntrend will hit sort of a plateau as highs stay in the mid 90s, \nbut cloud cover from storms has helped with cooler temperatures \nmoving in. By the end of the week, cooling temperature trends will\namp up with highs dropping into the 80s by the weekend. Southerly\nwinds will shift more to a southwesterly direction over the week \nas the high pressure shifts west, with elevated speeds up to 10-15\nmph, gusts 20-25 mph in the afternoons.\n\nAfter Wednesday, models show increasing confidence that the high\npressure that has been bringing in monsoonal moisture will begin\nto shift back west towards southern Arizona, thus cutting off\nmoisture flow. At the end of the week, the discrepancies with the\nmodels take shape with different outcomes of weather patterns.\nECMWF is showing the upper ridge strengthening and spreading\nacross the southwest, which will lead to a more southwesterly flow\nthat will give drier air and warmer temperatures. GFS is showing\nan upper level trough over the northwest and pushing towards the\nsilver state, giving a more zonal flow still with dry air but also with\ncooler temperatures. Confidence remains mixed on what the outcome\nwill be for the extended period with overall conditions expected\nto be dry, but temperatures and winds remain uncertain. \n\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence for daily\nmonsoon activity with a 20-50% chance today and tomorrow for\ncentral and northern Nevada. \n\nModerate confidence for a 10-30% chance for monsoon activity over\neastern Nevada Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nModerate confidence on dry conditions returning to the silver\nstate Thursday through Sunday next week.\n\nLow confidence in upper level patterns after Wednesday as models\nshow discrepancies on potential outcomes for winds and\ntemperatures.",
